1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,500
All right.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:07,000
All right.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,580
So last topic on behavioral economics part two,

4
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,000
behavioral economics and rationality.

5
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:15,680
All right.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:17,340
So I think I started on this a little bit,

7
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:21,120
but I'm giving this feel so many times all at once together.

8
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:24,300
Anyways, so as you may have noticed,

9
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:25,460
when you took an economics class,

10
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:28,140
it is likely that a professor tried to fix you.

11
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:29,140
All right.

12
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,140
So you showed up thinking some things about economics,

13
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:34,140
and then you had a professor who said,

14
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,140
you are a receptacle for economic garbage,

15
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:39,140
and the garbage must be taken out,

16
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,140
and you must be refilled with the light.

17
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:42,140
All right.

18
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:46,140
So at least that's kind of what happened to me,

19
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:48,140
and those were always the classes that I liked the most

20
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,140
and the books that I liked the most,

21
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:50,140
the ones that said, you know,

22
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,140
all the stuff that you were taught to believe

23
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:54,140
for your entire childhood,

24
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:55,140
and when they listed, I said, yes,

25
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:57,140
I was taught all of those things.

26
00:00:57,140 --> 00:00:59,140
Well, most of them are completely wrong,

27
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,140
or at least they are grotesque half-truths,

28
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,140
which must be amplified or augmented

29
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:05,140
in order to really understand what's going on,

30
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:07,140
and when I would listen to those arguments,

31
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:09,140
I would have this great sense of betrayal

32
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,140
that all of the adults of my life

33
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:12,140
had been withholding the truth,

34
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:13,140
had been preaching stuff

35
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,140
when they didn't know what they were talking about,

36
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,140
and also a sense of salvation.

37
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:19,140
Now I no longer am one of these people

38
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:22,140
from whom I came who believed nonsense.

39
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:24,140
Now I can be part of the special people

40
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,140
who believe good stuff,

41
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:27,140
and I will go forth into the world,

42
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,140
and I will preach the good word.

43
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:30,140
I'll settle on it.

44
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:33,140
This does not sound familiar at all to you.

45
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:36,140
Well, it's about time it did.

46
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,140
Anyway, so it is very common

47
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:40,140
in introductory econ classes

48
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:44,140
to try to correct the preexisting

49
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:45,140
systematically biased beliefs

50
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:47,140
about economics that students begin with,

51
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,140
the most famous one being

52
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:50,140
a course in international trade.

53
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,140
Normally, when economists teach international trade,

54
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,140
we go over to comparative advantage

55
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:55,140
and expect students to go,

56
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:59,140
oh my God, I've been so wrong my whole life.

57
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:01,140
And there's a good reason for that,

58
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:02,140
which is that comparative advantage

59
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:06,140
undermines almost the entire popular view

60
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:07,140
about international trade.

61
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:09,140
It really does.

62
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:14,140
And of course, this is not a new thing.

63
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:16,140
If you go back through the history of economics,

64
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,140
it is very common for economists

65
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:19,140
to spend time complaining about

66
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:21,140
the ridiculous things the public believes about economics.

67
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:24,140
So, Frederick Bastiat is the flag bearer

68
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:26,140
with his book Economic Sophisms,

69
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:27,140
which is all about, every chapter,

70
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:28,140
here's another ridiculous thing

71
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:30,140
most people think about economics.

72
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:31,140
Here's why it's totally wrong.

73
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:33,140
All right, never believe that again.

74
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:35,140
Next chapter, another ridiculous thing

75
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:36,140
people believe about economics,

76
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:37,140
also totally wrong.

77
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:39,140
Every chapter goes that way.

78
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:41,140
And Bastiat actually has a whole political economy

79
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:44,140
where he says, what's wrong with the world

80
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:46,140
is that the stuff that I'm teaching you

81
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:48,140
is known to hardly anybody,

82
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:51,140
and therefore policy is based upon error.

83
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:52,140
And not just random error,

84
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:55,140
but a very particular kind of shared error.

85
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:57,140
And if we could just go and wipe out the error,

86
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,140
we could make the world fantastic.

87
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:01,140
That's all it takes.

88
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,140
It is changing what everybody thinks.

89
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:05,140
That's all it is.

90
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:06,140
All right, so anyway,

91
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:07,140
many famous historical economists

92
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:08,140
operate from the same perspective.

93
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:09,140
You can read Adam Smith,

94
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:10,140
so this is before there's any kind of

95
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:12,140
public opinion research,

96
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:13,140
but Smith seems very convinced

97
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:16,140
that most people have populist views

98
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:19,140
about economics, which Smith tries to refute.

99
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,140
You can see this in, say,

100
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:22,140
Eugene von Babur or Frank Knight

101
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,140
or Milton Friedman or Paul Samuelson

102
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:26,140
or Paul Krugman from the 90s, right?

103
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:27,140
Or David Hume, right?

104
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:29,140
Paul Krugman in the 90s says,

105
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:31,140
the lessons that people need to learn

106
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:32,140
about international trade

107
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:34,140
aren't any of the fancy stuff I've been doing.

108
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:37,140
They need to learn the basics of Hume and Ricardo.

109
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:39,140
That's what he said.

110
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,140
All right, so anyway,

111
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:42,140
now, but what's weird

112
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:43,140
is that when you go and read

113
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,140
research papers on modern economics,

114
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,140
they normally assume that everybody

115
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,140
has rational expectations about economics.

116
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:52,140
So even though as teachers

117
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:54,140
we assume that our students are screwed up,

118
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:56,140
when we try to analyze the world,

119
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:57,140
we assume that people

120
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,140
who never even had the benefits

121
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:01,140
of our teachings are already okay.

122
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:04,140
They're already fine, at least on average.

123
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:05,140
So in other words,

124
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:07,140
when we go and model, for example,

125
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,140
public opinion about international trade,

126
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:10,140
we usually make the standard

127
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:12,140
rational expectations assumption,

128
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:14,140
which is that errors are going to be

129
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,140
normally or at least will be normally distributed,

130
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:18,140
and there will be a mean of zero

131
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:21,140
and a standard deviation of sigma squared.

132
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,140
I'm gonna say that.

133
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:24,140
So the error term will just be a normal,

134
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:26,140
normally distributed variable with zero means.

135
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:28,140
So on average, people's beliefs about trade

136
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,140
are neither overly pessimistic

137
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,140
nor overly optimistic.

138
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:34,140
And then people try to figure out

139
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,140
why we have protectionism

140
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,140
if people have accurate views about trade.

141
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:40,140
So of course, one answer would be

142
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:42,140
maybe economists are really wrong about trade

143
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:43,140
and trade is actually bad.

144
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,140
And people who've never seen the subject

145
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:47,140
somehow have figured it out.

146
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,140
Who's better than the experts?

147
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:53,140
So anyway, I have a series of empirical papers

148
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:56,140
that essentially try to test that assumption.

149
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,140
So I think I talked about it earlier in the semester,

150
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:00,140
but I'll talk about it now in more detail

151
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:03,140
since we have the right framework to think about it.

152
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:05,140
So the data set is the Survey of Americans

153
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:09,140
and Economists on the Economy, SAEE for short.

154
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,140
The idea of the data set is that

155
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:13,140
they ask exactly the same questions

156
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:16,140
to the general American public, randomly sampled,

157
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:21,140
and to PhD economists specializing in domestic policy.

158
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:23,140
So not going and talking to pure theorists

159
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:24,140
who don't know any facts,

160
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:27,140
but talking to actual domestic policy specialists.

161
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:31,140
Now, if we believe this rationalistic approach,

162
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:32,140
as I said before,

163
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:36,140
then if this is the true belief, P star,

164
00:05:36,140 --> 00:05:39,140
then the difference between experts and laymen

165
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,140
should be something like this.

166
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:44,140
The laymen should have a belief distribution like that,

167
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:48,140
and the experts should have a belief distribution like that.

168
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:51,140
But the mean of the two views should both be the same,

169
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:54,140
and namely the mean should be the actual truth,

170
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:57,140
the actual truth, what's really going on.

171
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,140
And then what the rationalistic assumption says,

172
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:04,140
in other words, is that if we go and test

173
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:07,140
the coefficient on the econ variable,

174
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:10,140
so the coefficient on an econ will be zero.

175
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:12,140
It will be zero.

176
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:13,140
So the, let's see,

177
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:15,140
let's do it like beta sub econ,

178
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:18,140
the beta in front of the econ dummy variable.

179
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:20,140
So you have a dummy variable,

180
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:25,140
which is equal to one if you're an economist,

181
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:28,140
and zero if you're not.

182
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,140
And then we can go and put that dummy variable

183
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:33,140
into regression and see, on average,

184
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:36,140
do economists and the public actually agree.

185
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:37,140
Now for this to really work,

186
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:39,140
you need to have questions that are descriptive.

187
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:42,140
So rational explanations is not usually seen

188
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:45,140
as a theory about what ought to be or what's good,

189
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:48,140
but rather a theory about what actually is the case.

190
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:50,140
Although, of course, for many questions,

191
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:53,140
it's a little bit, you have to think about

192
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:54,140
what the right meaning is.

193
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:55,140
For example, someone said,

194
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:58,140
is penicillin good for bacterial infections?

195
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:02,140
Is it reasonable to think of that as a normative claim?

196
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:05,140
Right, normally we take that as a descriptive claim,

197
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:08,140
saying penicillin cures bacterial infections.

198
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:13,140
It's not saying, I just feel that when someone has bacteria,

199
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:15,140
it is good to give them penicillin.

200
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:16,140
Why? I don't know.

201
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:17,140
It just is. It seems that way.

202
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:19,140
Right? The whole idea is that you're saying

203
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:21,140
it is good for a purpose,

204
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:23,140
and certainly you can empirically investigate

205
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:25,140
whether something is good for a purpose.

206
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:27,140
Though, if you wanted to say,

207
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:30,140
is it good to go and be nice to people,

208
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:33,140
then, well, that would normally be

209
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:37,140
a purely normative question about the goodness of niceness.

210
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:41,140
Common view is that it is good to be nice to people.

211
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,140
Right? Anyway.

212
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:45,140
Now, as I think I told you before,

213
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:47,140
there is a question, maybe we need to add

214
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:48,140
some control variables here

215
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:51,140
before we actually accept the results of the test,

216
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:54,140
because there are many people who think

217
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:56,140
that economists are actually wrong.

218
00:07:56,140 --> 00:07:58,140
Economists are actually mistaken,

219
00:07:58,140 --> 00:08:00,140
and maybe the public is right.

220
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:03,140
This is sort of the subtext of 2016.

221
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:05,140
All of these out-of-touch elites

222
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:07,140
didn't understand what regular people knew,

223
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,140
and then regular people voted for what they knew,

224
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,140
and now the experts are there going like,

225
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,140
what happened? I don't understand.

226
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:15,140
A common view of the world.

227
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:18,140
A view that almost no one wants to articulate is,

228
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:21,140
well, maybe what's going on is that the experts are well aware

229
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:23,140
the public doesn't know what it's doing,

230
00:08:23,140 --> 00:08:25,140
and in particular, the public is systematically biased,

231
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:28,140
and what happened in 2016 is the effort

232
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:30,140
to keep them from actually harming things

233
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:34,140
was reduced, and so the harm broke loose.

234
00:08:34,140 --> 00:08:36,140
So maybe that's the real story.

235
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,140
Of course, in a democracy, it's very unpopular to say,

236
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:40,140
well, here's the real story.

237
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:42,140
There's a 1% cognitive elite that understands the world,

238
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:46,140
99% of the benighted masses

239
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:48,140
who just need to shut up and take my class

240
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:49,140
and learn what's going on.

241
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:50,140
It's not unpopular.

242
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:52,140
Certainly no politician wants to say something like this.

243
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:54,140
No politician wants to say,

244
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:56,140
well, I know my views are out of touch,

245
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:58,140
but they're still true.

246
00:08:58,140 --> 00:09:00,140
So truth is lying.

247
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:03,140
And if you don't know some better way of selling

248
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:05,140
what's true to these people,

249
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:07,140
who haven't been able to figure it out yet, I'm open,

250
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:09,140
but what, that I should listen to them?

251
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:11,140
What do they know?

252
00:09:11,140 --> 00:09:16,140
Anyways, of course, that could be that the populist view is right,

253
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:19,140
but at least there is a possibility, of course,

254
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:22,140
that a simple story of the experts are right,

255
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:25,140
the laymen are wrong, and if the laymen really think that they're right,

256
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:27,140
that's just even worse.

257
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:29,140
Also possible.

258
00:09:29,140 --> 00:09:30,140
Alright.

259
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:33,140
So anyway, there are two main stories about why it is

260
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:36,140
that economists in particular should not be trusted.

261
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:41,140
The two main versions are, first of all, self-serving bias,

262
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:44,140
which says that economists are biased by their own self-interest,

263
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:46,140
so maybe they are rich,

264
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:48,140
or have a lot of job security,

265
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:51,140
and they imagine that whatever is good for them is good for the economy,

266
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:55,140
when in fact it's just good for people like economists.

267
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:58,140
And then another story is ideological bias.

268
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:04,140
This just says that economics is a place where radical free marketeers

269
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:06,140
hang out and talk to each other,

270
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:10,140
reinforce each other, scaring off anyone who disagrees.

