00:54:55,720 --> 00:54:59,000
If you always thought it was irrelevant, I don't know.

388
00:54:59,000 --> 00:55:02,240
All right, so moving on, so labor economics.

389
00:55:03,560 --> 00:55:05,960
All right, so starting, so human capital theory.

390
00:55:05,960 --> 00:55:07,920
So, I assume all of you guys remember

391
00:55:07,920 --> 00:55:09,760
calculating Presley's scan of values.

392
00:55:09,760 --> 00:55:14,440
PDVs, everyone remember Presley's scan of values?

393
00:55:14,440 --> 00:55:18,800
We try to figure out how much NASA will sell for today,

394
00:55:18,800 --> 00:55:21,400
if it's an income stream that pays in the future sometime.

395
00:55:21,400 --> 00:55:22,480
Right?

396
00:55:22,480 --> 00:55:24,200
That also called Necker's of value?

397
00:55:24,200 --> 00:55:27,760
Yes, I'm not getting the usual books of recognition.

398
00:55:27,760 --> 00:55:30,960
All right, so I offer you a dollar a year from now,

399
00:55:30,960 --> 00:55:32,640
will you pay a dollar for that?

400
00:55:32,640 --> 00:55:34,480
Of course not, right, because of

401
00:55:34,480 --> 00:55:36,360
the opportunity cost of interest.

402
00:55:36,360 --> 00:55:39,000
All right, so and remember,

403
00:55:39,000 --> 00:55:41,640
the lower interest rates, the future benefits count more.

404
00:55:41,640 --> 00:55:43,080
Right, so if you got 100% interest rate,

405
00:55:43,080 --> 00:55:44,520
then stuff happening a few years in the future

406
00:55:44,520 --> 00:55:46,080
counts for pretty much nothing.

407
00:55:46,920 --> 00:55:50,280
All right, now, PDVs are mostly used for businesses,

408
00:55:50,280 --> 00:55:51,400
but the idea is totally general,

409
00:55:51,400 --> 00:55:54,960
you can do the PDV of adding insulation to your house,

410
00:55:54,960 --> 00:55:56,680
for example, and of course,

411
00:55:56,680 --> 00:55:58,880
you can do the PDV of attending school,

412
00:55:58,880 --> 00:56:00,000
which I assume everyone did here

413
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:01,720
before they bothered applying to grad school,

414
00:56:01,720 --> 00:56:03,240
is you put down your spreadsheet,

415
00:56:03,240 --> 00:56:04,640
looked at interest rate forecasts,

416
00:56:04,640 --> 00:56:07,560
and came up with comparative income stream analysis,

417
00:56:07,560 --> 00:56:08,960
and you get all this stuff.

418
00:56:10,400 --> 00:56:13,680
All right, so anyway, this idea that you can apply the PDV

419
00:56:13,680 --> 00:56:18,680
to educational decisions and to labor decisions in general

420
00:56:19,480 --> 00:56:22,160
is the heart of human capital theory.

421
00:56:22,160 --> 00:56:24,360
You can think about your labor market decisions

422
00:56:24,360 --> 00:56:26,280
like any other investment.

423
00:56:26,280 --> 00:56:28,160
So you get enough to go to school,

424
00:56:28,160 --> 00:56:30,280
then the obvious thing is to add up the PDV

425
00:56:30,280 --> 00:56:31,600
of your foregone earnings during school,

426
00:56:31,600 --> 00:56:35,320
and the extra income you expect to get after schooling,

427
00:56:35,320 --> 00:56:38,480
and then, of course, since when you're going to school,

428
00:56:38,480 --> 00:56:39,880
when do you lose the money?

429
00:56:41,080 --> 00:56:42,680
When do you lose money when you go to school?

430
00:56:42,680 --> 00:56:43,680
When you pay the tuition.

431
00:56:43,680 --> 00:56:45,320
When you're in school, you lose the money, the tuition,

432
00:56:45,320 --> 00:56:46,800
but also the foregone earnings.

433
00:56:46,800 --> 00:56:47,640
Oh, that too.

434
00:56:47,640 --> 00:56:49,120
Very important, right?

435
00:56:49,120 --> 00:56:51,880
And on the other hand, when do you generally get the money?

436
00:56:51,880 --> 00:56:52,720
After graduation.

437
00:56:52,720 --> 00:56:53,960
Yeah, after graduation.

438
00:56:53,960 --> 00:56:55,880
So in that case, by this reasoning,

439
00:56:55,880 --> 00:56:58,080
when interest rates are higher,

