00:59:58,960 --> 01:00:24,880
And, so.


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
In some sense, I feel like I have a greater breadth of knowledge than any other economist on this,

2
01:00:06,000 --> 01:00:12,000
because normally when you're trying to publish a paper, you go and do one tweak and then you recalculate the present discounted value.

3
01:00:12,000 --> 01:00:16,000
Whereas I tried to take all the tweaks and put them together at the same time.

4
01:00:16,000 --> 01:00:18,000
Because I was trying to give people useful advice.

5
01:00:18,000 --> 01:00:25,000
If you want to give useful advice, you take all the complications that matter and put it all together and then give the people an answer.

6
01:00:25,000 --> 01:00:28,000
Whereas if you just want to publish a paper, what's the point of that?

7
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:32,000
If you just want to publish a paper, you just do one very technically sophisticated tweak,

8
01:00:32,000 --> 01:00:35,000
and then you write another paper and just start from scratch and do a different tweak.

9
01:00:35,000 --> 01:00:46,000
So anyway, I tried to go and take all the research and make it relevant for both personal educational decisions as well as for public policy.

10
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:49,000
Which almost no economist does.

11
01:00:49,000 --> 01:00:55,000
Sometimes people think tanks will go and do present discounted value of the sort,

12
01:00:55,000 --> 01:01:00,000
but normally they do a really crummy job where they'll do like three complications and then they're done.

13
01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:09,000
Why would you do a policy analysis with three complications and then declare victory when there's a thousand research papers on the topic?

14
01:01:09,000 --> 01:01:13,000
Well, what happens to a policy analysis with a thousand complications in it?

15
01:01:13,000 --> 01:01:15,000
No one wants to read that thing.

16
01:01:15,000 --> 01:01:21,000
And then, you know, part A, three, sub-circuit through alpha, delta, alright?

17
01:01:21,000 --> 01:01:25,000
That's pretty boring to most people. So people would rather get simple advice,

18
01:01:25,000 --> 01:01:29,000
which is actually not really taking into account a bunch of important factors.

19
01:01:29,000 --> 01:01:35,000
So I do try to go and narrow down the factors that really change the result a lot versus the ones that don't change very much.

20
01:01:35,000 --> 01:01:46,000
But I will say that my judgment is all the main think tank pieces published on the private and social returns to education ignore multiple huge problems.

21
01:01:46,000 --> 01:01:51,000
And the biggest one of all is just completion probability.

22
01:01:51,000 --> 01:01:57,000
Almost all policy analysis just figure out how much education pays by looking at the people who graduated.

23
01:01:57,000 --> 01:02:02,000
Which is a lot like figuring out whether loans are good by looking at loans that are repaid.

24
01:02:02,000 --> 01:02:09,000
No. If you're figuring out whether a loan is profitable, you must consider the loans that don't get repaid.

25
01:02:09,000 --> 01:02:15,000
In fact, that is probably the single most important predictor of profitability of lending money is figuring,

26
01:02:15,000 --> 01:02:18,000
is the share of bad loans.

27
01:02:18,000 --> 01:02:25,000
So remember, if you've got loans of 5% interest, but 5% of the loans are not repaid at all,

28
01:02:25,000 --> 01:02:28,000
then approximately you're making nothing on those loans on average.

29
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:33,000
And then, say, well, is non-completion of school really a big deal?

30
01:02:33,000 --> 01:02:38,000
Let's see, well, for high school it's about 80% completion rate.

31
01:02:38,000 --> 01:02:40,000
That's not bad, is it?

32
01:02:40,000 --> 01:02:43,000
Well, if loans had an 80% repayment rate, you'd consider that pretty bad, right?

33
01:02:43,000 --> 01:02:47,000
That would be sub-junk. It would be terrible.

34
01:02:47,000 --> 01:02:53,000
And then for college, the completion rate is way lower than that.

35
01:02:53,000 --> 01:03:03,000
So the four-year completion rate for full-time students, like doing bachelor's degrees, is about 35%.

36
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:07,000
So five-year completion rate for a four-year program goes up to 55%.

37
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:13,000
But think about this, well, suppose that in reality you spend five years doing a four-year program,

38
01:03:13,000 --> 01:03:18,000
what does that, to a rough approximation, do to your rate of return?

39
01:03:18,000 --> 01:03:21,000
If you actually have to pay five years of investment to get a four-year pay-off?

40
01:03:21,000 --> 01:03:22,000
It decreases it.

41
01:03:22,000 --> 01:03:24,000
Yes. How much does it decrease it by?

42
01:03:24,000 --> 01:03:25,000
An additional year.

43
01:03:25,000 --> 01:03:31,000
Yeah, so you're dividing by five instead of four when you're looking at the costs.

44
01:03:31,000 --> 01:03:36,000
So yeah, so dividing by five rather than four, what does that do?

45
01:03:36,000 --> 01:03:37,000
Well, let's see.

46
01:03:37,000 --> 01:03:43,000
Well, I believe that reduces the rate of return by 20%, not 20 percentage points, but 20%.

47
01:03:43,000 --> 01:03:44,000
All right.

48
01:03:44,000 --> 01:03:47,000
So anyway, enormous literature.

49
01:03:47,000 --> 01:03:50,000
The underlying motivation of this literature.

50
01:03:50,000 --> 01:03:56,000
Many economists think that there are big credit market imperfections in the educational loan markets.

51
01:03:56,000 --> 01:04:05,000
So that's what's driving all of these estimates.

52
01:04:05,000 --> 01:04:10,000
So no economist bothers going and calculating the present-day standard value of investments in the steel industry.

53
01:04:10,000 --> 01:04:12,000
That's for the people in steel to do.

54
01:04:12,000 --> 01:04:17,000
And the background assumption is economists saying, look, if there were profitable investments for a steel mill,

55
01:04:17,000 --> 01:04:21,000
and the steel mill didn't have the cash to do it, what would the steel mill obviously do?

56
01:04:21,000 --> 01:04:22,000
Borrow.

57
01:04:22,000 --> 01:04:23,000
Go and borrow.

58
01:04:23,000 --> 01:04:28,000
And they would easily be able to get a loan because they've got what is collateral?

59
01:04:28,000 --> 01:04:29,000
The mill.

60
01:04:29,000 --> 01:04:30,000
All right.

61
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:31,000
So there's no problem there.

62
01:04:31,000 --> 01:04:36,000
So for most businesses, economists are very cavalierly just saying the free market will take care of us.

63
01:04:36,000 --> 01:04:37,000
Right.

64
01:04:37,000 --> 01:04:42,000
But for education, economists have a sense of, no, no, no, no, it doesn't work that way in education.

65
01:04:42,000 --> 01:04:46,000
Because in education, if someone could very well have a great educational opportunity,

66
01:04:46,000 --> 01:04:51,000
but they don't have the money for it, and it would be very hard for them to get a loan in order to do it,

67
01:04:51,000 --> 01:04:56,000
because what collateral does a student got?

68
01:04:56,000 --> 01:04:57,000
Nothing.

69
01:04:57,000 --> 01:04:59,000
You can't do a debt-reserve-to-contracts.

70
01:04:59,000 --> 01:05:04,000
So you can go and take a big educational loan and then go and blow it all and then say,

71
01:05:04,000 --> 01:05:06,000
dude, I just don't have the money.

72
01:05:06,000 --> 01:05:09,000
And then lenders are not going to be very interested in lending you the money.

73
01:05:09,000 --> 01:05:10,000
Okay.

74
01:05:10,000 --> 01:05:11,000
All right.

75
01:05:11,000 --> 01:05:14,000
So now, of course, this is only a suspicion.

76
01:05:14,000 --> 01:05:20,000
So normally what education economists are doing is going and calculating the return to education.

77
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:27,000
In other words, seeing what interest rate you would need to make the present discounted value of going

78
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:29,000
and not going equal.

79
01:05:29,000 --> 01:05:30,000
Why is that?

80
01:05:30,000 --> 01:05:39,000
Well, so suppose that you've got an asset, let's see, you can either go and buy an asset at its present value,

81
01:05:39,000 --> 01:05:43,000
and so let's see, let's put the right word in.

82
01:05:43,000 --> 01:05:49,000
So suppose that you've got perfect capital markets, and you have an opportunity to buy an asset at its present value.

83
01:05:49,000 --> 01:05:51,000
Is that a good deal?

84
01:05:51,000 --> 01:05:59,000
Well, if you've got perfect capital markets, then if you go and you pay the present value,

85
01:05:59,000 --> 01:06:05,000
and then you go and get the amount that you paid times the interest rate a year later,

86
01:06:05,000 --> 01:06:07,000
and what's the net effect?

87
01:06:07,000 --> 01:06:09,000
Zero.

88
01:06:09,000 --> 01:06:16,000
So if, say, there's a 5% interest rate, and there's 5% interest, and then you go and you buy an asset,

89
01:06:16,000 --> 01:06:18,000
and there's a bond for $1,000.

90
01:06:18,000 --> 01:06:22,000
So with 5% interest, a bond today will sell for approximately what?

91
01:06:22,000 --> 01:06:24,000
$950, right?

92
01:06:24,000 --> 01:06:26,000
So it's approximately $950.

93
01:06:26,000 --> 01:06:28,000
So $950.

94
01:06:28,000 --> 01:06:32,000
And if you just took the $950 and put it in the bank at 5%, what would you have at the end of the year?

95
01:06:32,000 --> 01:06:34,000
$1,000.

96
01:06:34,000 --> 01:06:39,000
So here we go, and we say, well, we go and we pay $950, and then what do we get in exchange?

97
01:06:39,000 --> 01:06:45,000
We get $1,000 divided by 1.05, and that's basically equal to zero.

98
01:06:45,000 --> 01:06:50,000
There's a little rounding error there, so you have to do the math a little bit more precisely to get over it.

99
01:06:50,000 --> 01:06:52,000
All right, that's the idea.

100
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:55,000
Okay, now how do you calculate return to education?

101
01:06:55,000 --> 01:06:59,000
Well, there are some big simplifications which are commonly used.

102
01:06:59,000 --> 01:07:02,000
There's nothing wrong with simplifications as long as you remember what you're doing

103
01:07:02,000 --> 01:07:10,000
and then correct for them before you give anyone some advice that actually would be a total disaster, unfortunately.

104
01:07:10,000 --> 01:07:18,000
It's very common for education economists, when they talk to journalists, to forget conveniently what the key qualifications are

105
01:07:18,000 --> 01:07:23,000
and then give a number that actually is not one that you should act upon.

106
01:07:23,000 --> 01:07:27,000
In any case, the standard simplifications are as follows.

107
01:07:27,000 --> 01:07:34,000
So first of all, we go and we assume that the only cost of education is foregone earnings.

108
01:07:34,000 --> 01:07:39,000
So it's very common that economists assume that the only thing you give up in school is full-time earnings

109
01:07:39,000 --> 01:07:41,000
that you would have had if you were working.

110
01:07:41,000 --> 01:07:46,000
So in other words, it is common to just ignore tuition, set it at zero.

111
01:07:46,000 --> 01:07:48,000
Okay, all right.

112
01:07:48,000 --> 01:07:54,000
Now then, if we ignore the fact that people eventually die, ignore the fact that people eventually die,

113
01:07:54,000 --> 01:07:59,000
actually this one is, in present value terms, is actually a pretty small simplification

114
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:06,000
because if you think about what is the present value of all your income from 40 years in the future to infinity,

115
01:08:06,000 --> 01:08:10,000
it's actually a pretty small number because you've got 40 years worth of interest.

116
01:08:10,000 --> 01:08:15,000
Divide by the interest rate raised to the 40th power, then divide the next thing by the interest rate raised to the 41st power,

117
01:08:15,000 --> 01:08:17,000
and so on, that's a pretty small amount.

118
01:08:17,000 --> 01:08:25,000
In any case, if we go and ignore finite lifespan, then we can simply go and do a regression of log earnings on a constant

119
01:08:25,000 --> 01:08:29,000
and years of education, and then we can get our return of education.

120
01:08:29,000 --> 01:08:38,000
So we can do the log of earnings, which will equal a constant, plus beta times years of education,

121
01:08:38,000 --> 01:08:42,000
so that's education in years.

122
01:08:42,000 --> 01:08:49,000
And if we assume, counterfactually, that the only cost of education is foregone earnings,

123
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:54,000
then this beta will be your return to education.

124
01:08:54,000 --> 01:08:57,000
Why? Well, suppose that beta is.1.

125
01:08:57,000 --> 01:09:02,000
This says that if we get a year of education, that will raise our earnings by what percent?

126
01:09:02,000 --> 01:09:04,000
Ten percent.

127
01:09:04,000 --> 01:09:08,000
And what does this mean? It means that you give up one year of earnings,

128
01:09:08,000 --> 01:09:12,000
and then for the rest of your life, you make ten percent more every year.

129
01:09:12,000 --> 01:09:18,000
That sounds a lot like a famous British financial instrument, the console.

130
01:09:18,000 --> 01:09:23,000
So remember the idea of a console is that it pays a dollar a year forever.

131
01:09:23,000 --> 01:09:30,000
So if you go and you pay ten dollars for your console, then you never get the money back.

132
01:09:30,000 --> 01:09:33,000
So ten dollars gone, and then you just get a dollar a year forever.

133
01:09:33,000 --> 01:09:37,000
That is an interest rate of approximately what?

134
01:09:37,000 --> 01:09:39,000
Ten percent.

135
01:09:39,000 --> 01:09:41,000
Yes, ten percent. Right.

136
01:09:41,000 --> 01:09:44,000
So that is an interest rate of ten percent.

137
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:49,000
So basically you pay ten dollars, and every year you get ten percent of that.

138
01:09:49,000 --> 01:09:52,000
Similarly, this is saying you give up one year's worth of education,

139
01:09:52,000 --> 01:09:57,000
and then every year after that you are making ten percent extra.

140
01:09:57,000 --> 01:10:00,000
So just like a console.

141
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:03,000
And of course, the Aveda were.05.

142
01:10:03,000 --> 01:10:06,000
This would be saying that every year of education raises your earnings by five percent.

143
01:10:06,000 --> 01:10:11,000
So you have no income for one year, and then after that you earn what you would have earned before,

144
01:10:11,000 --> 01:10:16,000
with a bonus of five percent on top, forever.

145
01:10:16,000 --> 01:10:22,000
Now, if you go and use the NLSY, the National Longitudinal Study of Youth,

146
01:10:22,000 --> 01:10:26,000
in some ways the most amazing data set on labor economics ever,

147
01:10:26,000 --> 01:10:30,000
divided whether the General Social Survey or the NLSY is the most amazing.

148
01:10:30,000 --> 01:10:33,000
The General Social Survey is more amazing for the diversity of the questions.

149
01:10:33,000 --> 01:10:39,000
The NLSY, for the determination of the researchers to hunt down the participants for the rest of their life,

150
01:10:39,000 --> 01:10:43,000
as well as their children, and their children's children.

151
01:10:43,000 --> 01:10:48,000
It is a multi-generational survey. If you were in it, they try to put your kids in it, and they can be in it too.

152
01:10:48,000 --> 01:10:57,000
So if you go and do this regression using the NLSY, you get a value of beta equal to something like.126.

153
01:10:57,000 --> 01:11:00,000
Now this is controlled for nothing at all.

154
01:11:00,000 --> 01:11:03,000
This is just for a really simple regression.

155
01:11:03,000 --> 01:11:05,000
That's what you get.

156
01:11:05,000 --> 01:11:10,000
Now this number is surely too high.

157
01:11:10,000 --> 01:11:15,000
I'm scrapping up my book, so I think I'm going to revise these notes.

158
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:18,000
I always knew that there were a bunch of problems with these numbers.

159
01:11:18,000 --> 01:11:21,000
Now there are problems worse than I had ever thought.

160
01:11:21,000 --> 01:11:26,000
So by the end, I'm like, oh my god, there's so much mess just to clean up, just to get started.

161
01:11:26,000 --> 01:11:28,000
But anyway, so some of the problems.

162
01:11:28,000 --> 01:11:30,000
One of them is, in the real world, do you live forever?

163
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:32,000
I can never remember.

164
01:11:32,000 --> 01:11:33,000
Of course not.

165
01:11:33,000 --> 01:11:35,000
Man, I wanted to live forever.

166
01:11:35,000 --> 01:11:38,000
So you don't live forever, and you work for even less time than forever.

167
01:11:38,000 --> 01:11:40,000
So it's the role of retire.

168
01:11:40,000 --> 01:11:43,000
So anyway, you don't reap the benefits of increased education forever.

169
01:11:43,000 --> 01:11:49,000
Now this is actually a very slight effect, because those lost years are far in the future.

170
01:11:49,000 --> 01:11:54,000
So if someone offers to pay you $1.40 a year from now, it's really not worth very much.

171
01:11:54,000 --> 01:12:00,000
And of course, if we have these high interest rates, then those years are worth almost nothing.

172
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:04,000
So doing that, there's only an imperceptible drop in rate of return.

173
01:12:04,000 --> 01:12:08,000
It brings you down to like 12.56 from 12.6, so it's microscopic.

174
01:12:08,000 --> 01:12:12,000
All right, but then we start getting into the really big problems.

175
01:12:12,000 --> 01:12:13,000
Here's one.

176
01:12:13,000 --> 01:12:14,000
There is tuition.

177
01:12:14,000 --> 01:12:17,000
Tuition does exist.

178
01:12:17,000 --> 01:12:25,000
Now you might think, yeah, well, that's obvious, especially given that economists are kind of doing this stuff to argue for tuition subsidies.

179
01:12:25,000 --> 01:12:30,000
So there's something very strange about assuming that tuition doesn't exist, coming up with a really high number,

180
01:12:30,000 --> 01:12:33,000
and then using it to justify a tuition subsidy.

181
01:12:33,000 --> 01:12:35,000
But people do it.

182
01:12:35,000 --> 01:12:36,000
So very strange.

183
01:12:36,000 --> 01:12:38,000
So we need a cost-free service to educate people.

184
01:12:38,000 --> 01:12:42,000
And if you count these costs, then it does drastically reduce the rate of return.

185
01:12:42,000 --> 01:12:48,000
So like if you've got an annual tuition of $15,000, then that drops the return by about 50%,

186
01:12:48,000 --> 01:12:51,000
so from like 12.6 to about 6.5.

187
01:12:51,000 --> 01:12:55,000
So that makes an enormous dent in the result.

188
01:12:55,000 --> 01:12:56,000
All right, then another problem.

189
01:12:56,000 --> 01:12:58,000
So there's also return to experience.

190
01:12:58,000 --> 01:13:06,000
So your option is not actually go to school and get an increase in your earnings or work and get no increase in your earnings.

191
01:13:06,000 --> 01:13:11,000
Instead, there's a whole literature saying that working amazingly raises your earnings, too.

192
01:13:11,000 --> 01:13:17,000
So you want to go and actually make an adjustment for the lost experience that you are giving up when you're in school.

193
01:13:17,000 --> 01:13:20,000
So there's that, too.

194
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:31,000
Another problem is that that 12.6% that we're looking at is, like all ordinary least squares regressions, an estimate of an average effect.

195
01:13:31,000 --> 01:13:36,000
But should you go and base your life decisions upon averages?

196
01:13:36,000 --> 01:13:37,000
No.

197
01:13:37,000 --> 01:13:38,000
No?

198
01:13:38,000 --> 01:13:39,000
You should base them on marginal things.

199
01:13:39,000 --> 01:13:40,000
Marginal.

200
01:13:40,000 --> 01:13:41,000
Right?

201
01:13:41,000 --> 01:13:46,000
And there is a standard argument about why we should expect that marginal people will benefit less than average people.

202
01:13:46,000 --> 01:13:54,000
Like imagine that you are the marginal person who's thinking about going to the next Star Wars movie.

203
01:13:54,000 --> 01:13:59,000
Are you likely to enjoy that movie as much as the people who know for sure they have to be there on opening day?

204
01:13:59,000 --> 01:14:00,000
No.

205
01:14:00,000 --> 01:14:01,000
No.

206
01:14:01,000 --> 01:14:02,000
Right?

207
01:14:02,000 --> 01:14:03,000
The people are like, eh, it's all right, I guess.

208
01:14:03,000 --> 01:14:05,000
Is there anything else that I can be doing that day?

209
01:14:05,000 --> 01:14:11,000
Those people are very likely to get less value for the product.

210
01:14:11,000 --> 01:14:17,000
Similarly, like the marginal person at a restaurant, the marginal person who goes on a trip, the marginal person who wants to show.

211
01:14:17,000 --> 01:14:21,000
All these people are normally getting less value than the average person.

212
01:14:21,000 --> 01:14:27,000
So it makes sense also to think that the marginal person that is going to school is going to be getting less value.

213
01:14:27,000 --> 01:14:35,000
Now this is particularly clear because, let's see, so, actually I'm going to get to the next one.

214
01:14:35,000 --> 01:14:36,000
So anyway, so that's one.

215
01:14:36,000 --> 01:14:38,000
So you can think about this.

216
01:14:38,000 --> 01:14:45,000
So the person who is just on the edge of going to college, what kinds of majors do they generally not pursue if they happen to go?

217
01:14:45,000 --> 01:14:48,000
Engineering, computer science.

218
01:14:48,000 --> 01:14:53,000
So people who do those degrees generally knew they were going to college.

219
01:14:53,000 --> 01:15:00,000
Whereas if you're like, eh, I don't know if college is for me, well be a computer scientist or electrical engineer.

220
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:01,000
I don't think so.

221
01:15:01,000 --> 01:15:07,000
All right, so those are fields where you need to have really good math scores and people with really good math scores almost always go to college.

222
01:15:07,000 --> 01:15:11,000
Instead it's likely to be a weaker student who's going to go and do an easier major.

223
01:15:11,000 --> 01:15:14,000
And easier majors generally don't pay very well.

224
01:15:14,000 --> 01:15:18,000
Except for economics.

225
01:15:18,000 --> 01:15:23,000
Yes, economics is the great outlier barely behind intellectual engineering and computer science and earnings.

226
01:15:23,000 --> 01:15:28,000
But per hour of work, we crush those people.

227
01:15:28,000 --> 01:15:29,000
Like, they're suckers.

228
01:15:29,000 --> 01:15:33,000
I don't know what they're doing.

229
01:15:33,000 --> 01:15:41,000
When I tell this to undergraduates and I say, look, economics is the highest paid of all the easy majors, there's like a rumbling of discontent and insult.

230
01:15:41,000 --> 01:15:43,000
They're like, come on, people.

231
01:15:43,000 --> 01:15:49,000
You don't disappear into the depths of the earth for weeks at a time emerging with vitamin D deficiency.

232
01:15:49,000 --> 01:15:51,000
But that's what computer scientists do.

233
01:15:51,000 --> 01:15:54,000
That's their life.

234
01:15:54,000 --> 01:15:59,000
When I was an undergraduate at Berkeley, they actually did have an underground basement for the computer lab.

235
01:15:59,000 --> 01:16:03,000
They really would just disappear down there for days, not seeing the sun for days at a time.

236
01:16:03,000 --> 01:16:07,000
And come up blinking like owls.

237
01:16:07,000 --> 01:16:11,000
So no economist suffers like that.

238
01:16:11,000 --> 01:16:15,000
In Toronto, the engineers, let's say, go to something called the dungeon.

239
01:16:15,000 --> 01:16:18,000
They're killing themselves.

240
01:16:18,000 --> 01:16:22,000
If you become an undergrad, you still get to have the full college experience.

241
01:16:22,000 --> 01:16:23,000
Great deal.

242
01:16:23,000 --> 01:16:26,000
I don't need to convince you guys of that.

243
01:16:26,000 --> 01:16:27,000
You're already here.

244
01:16:27,000 --> 01:16:31,000
This is like preaching the need to attend church to people who are in church.

245
01:16:31,000 --> 01:16:33,000
I have no need.

246
01:16:33,000 --> 01:16:34,000
All right.

247
01:16:34,000 --> 01:16:48,000
So now another big problem with this, and actually this is one that is enormous, and I knew it was a problem, but I just didn't realize how big the problem was until I looked at the data, is that this tacitly assumes that school completion probability is 100%.

248
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:57,000
That's the tacit assumption, because remember what you're doing is you're looking at the earnings of people who successfully completed the degree.

249
01:16:57,000 --> 01:17:00,000
That's what you do when you're pointing, that's what this does.

250
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:07,000
It goes and says, all right, because the question actually specifically says what is the highest number of years that you successfully completed?

251
01:17:07,000 --> 01:17:09,000
That's the question warning.

252
01:17:09,000 --> 01:17:12,000
If they had done, what is the total number of years you attempted?

253
01:17:12,000 --> 01:17:14,000
That would be a different answer.

254
01:17:14,000 --> 01:17:22,000
Probably people would just be so embarrassed about all their failure that they might not even tell you, but we've got other data saying that this failure is rampant.

255
01:17:22,000 --> 01:17:25,000
So, and then you're like, oh, I'm not, you're finishing bachelor's degrees.

256
01:17:25,000 --> 01:17:28,000
Remember, this is for the full-time students.

257
01:17:28,000 --> 01:17:40,000
Full-time four-year students, 35% finish in four years, 55% by five years, 60 by six years, and then you're just crawling up at 1% or 2% a year after that.

258
01:17:40,000 --> 01:17:44,000
So if you don't finish in six years, you're pretty much never finishing.

259
01:17:44,000 --> 01:17:50,000
So that is a whole lot of people who are putting in a whole lot of years and then not making it, not finishing.

260
01:17:50,000 --> 01:17:53,000
All right.

261
01:17:53,000 --> 01:18:05,000
And actually, just to get a little bit ahead of myself, this is even worse than it seems, because one very well-established empirical fact about the course of education is that there is what is called a Sheepskin effect,

262
01:18:05,000 --> 01:18:14,000
or diploma effect, which says that much of the payoff for education comes from the last year, finishing the last year.

263
01:18:14,000 --> 01:18:18,000
Graduation years pay a lot more than regular years.

264
01:18:18,000 --> 01:18:25,000
So for my book, I actually tracked down every single paper I could find on this and then just took an average.

265
01:18:25,000 --> 01:18:39,000
So there are big ranges, but for high school, the payoff for senior year comes out on average, senior year pays about 3.5 times as much as a regular year.

266
01:18:39,000 --> 01:18:45,000
So in other words, about a little bit more than half of the payoff for high school comes from finishing senior year.

267
01:18:45,000 --> 01:18:51,000
For the bachelor's degree, it's like a ratio of 6.7 to 1.

268
01:18:51,000 --> 01:18:57,000
Graduation year pays about double what all the first three years pay combined.

269
01:18:57,000 --> 01:19:05,000
That means that failing to graduate is a really serious problem, because you can go and do the first three years and you get peanuts for those years.

270
01:19:05,000 --> 01:19:08,000
You get almost nothing for the first three years of college.

271
01:19:08,000 --> 01:19:12,000
And then, if you finish, then you go and you get a big bag of gold at the end.

272
01:19:12,000 --> 01:19:28,000
But if a whole lot of people actually never finish, never cross that line, then to go and take the wages of the people who succeeded and act as if that's going to happen to everybody who tries is not just wrong.

273
01:19:28,000 --> 01:19:31,000
It's worse than misleading. It's cruel.

274
01:19:31,000 --> 01:19:40,000
Because you're going and painting these amazing dreams of what will happen after you finish without ever mentioning, oh, by the way, people like you hardly ever finish.

275
01:19:40,000 --> 01:19:45,000
How predictable is college completion? Very predictable.

276
01:19:45,000 --> 01:19:47,000
What do you think predicts college completion?

277
01:19:47,000 --> 01:19:49,000
Besides intelligence?

278
01:19:49,000 --> 01:19:51,000
Yes. Well, that's the first one, yes, intelligence.

279
01:19:51,000 --> 01:20:12,000
Again, a lot of people never actually get an IQ test for them, but you just have your standard psychological learning.


1
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:02,000
The best predictor of future performance is?

2
01:20:02,920 --> 01:20:05,700
So best predictor of success in college is success in?

3
01:20:05,700 --> 01:20:06,500
High school.

4
01:20:06,500 --> 01:20:07,720
And that works really well.

5
01:20:07,720 --> 01:20:11,020
So like out of people who are only about the bottom 25%

6
01:20:11,020 --> 01:20:12,340
of high school math test scorers,

7
01:20:12,720 --> 01:20:15,140
only about 5% finish college if they start.

8
01:20:16,480 --> 01:20:17,940
Of course, most of you like that don't even start.

9
01:20:17,940 --> 01:20:20,460
But if they do, they have almost no chance of finishing.

10
01:20:22,860 --> 01:20:24,080
So, shocking.

11
01:20:24,580 --> 01:20:26,400
All right, now last point, last point.

12
01:20:26,960 --> 01:20:28,440
Also a very serious omission.

13
01:20:28,440 --> 01:20:31,040
This number does not control for intelligence,

14
01:20:31,400 --> 01:20:33,440
which is highly correlated with education.

15
01:20:34,200 --> 01:20:36,980
And furthermore, is fairly stable from a young age.

16
01:20:36,980 --> 01:20:38,600
So the reverse causation story

17
01:20:38,600 --> 01:20:40,000
of the education made you smarter,

18
01:20:40,460 --> 01:20:42,100
while there is something to it,

19
01:20:42,100 --> 01:20:44,840
is not a very good objection, I guess.

20
01:20:44,840 --> 01:20:49,100
Have you looked at a sheepskin effect for PhDs?

21
01:20:49,100 --> 01:20:53,000
So, just to be clear, I'm not doing any of this research.

22
01:20:53,000 --> 01:20:55,000
I'm literally looking.

23
01:20:55,000 --> 01:20:56,440
I'm literally looking at what other people have done.

24
01:20:56,440 --> 01:21:01,000
So, yes, so PhDs specifically have not been studied very much,

25
01:21:01,000 --> 01:21:03,280
but there are several studies of just graduate

26
01:21:03,280 --> 01:21:04,660
to graduate and professional degrees.

27
01:21:05,160 --> 01:21:06,600
And yes, very standard defined,

28
01:21:06,600 --> 01:21:08,300
very large sheepskin effects for those.

29
01:21:08,700 --> 01:21:11,040
In fact, according to some estimates, the entire payoff

30
01:21:11,040 --> 01:21:12,600
for graduate degrees is a sheepskin effect.

31
01:21:13,000 --> 01:21:15,500
Even more extreme, where it's like 100% sheepskin possibly.

32
01:21:16,360 --> 01:21:19,240
So like working on a PhD for five years and then giving up,

33
01:21:19,540 --> 01:21:21,240
it's not clear the world rewards that at all.

34
01:21:23,120 --> 01:21:24,460
If you finish the master's along the way,

35
01:21:24,460 --> 01:21:25,620
then you get rewarded for the master's.

