65
01:42:28,600 --> 01:42:30,440
So Richard Feynman supposedly got a low score

66
01:42:30,440 --> 01:42:31,400
in an IQ test once.

67
01:42:31,400 --> 01:42:34,400
I've heard this repeated many times as to what it means.

68
01:42:35,020 --> 01:42:37,200
So if it's a test where there's a penalty for guessing,

69
01:42:37,620 --> 01:42:38,920
then actually you'd have to be a genius

70
01:42:38,920 --> 01:42:41,220
to get the minimum score, right?

71
01:42:41,880 --> 01:42:44,800
So because, you know, it's a regular test

72
01:42:44,800 --> 01:42:45,980
where you get marked out for guessing.

73
01:42:46,320 --> 01:42:48,720
Now how could you get the wrong answer every time?

74
01:42:48,720 --> 01:42:50,220
Only if you knew the right answer

75
01:42:50,220 --> 01:42:51,560
and deliberately did the opposite.

76
01:42:51,560 --> 01:42:54,860
So it would take a very smart person to min

77
01:42:54,860 --> 01:42:56,940
out their score on a test like that.

78
01:42:56,940 --> 01:42:59,420
Of course, it doesn't take any intelligence at all

79
01:42:59,420 --> 01:43:00,620
to just leave the whole exam blank.

80
01:43:01,020 --> 01:43:02,980
The real genius is getting a negative score.

81
01:43:02,980 --> 01:43:06,620
Moderate negative score might not prove anything,

82
01:43:06,620 --> 01:43:09,920
but if you get a really negative score, that's a challenge.

83
01:43:09,920 --> 01:43:10,820
All right.

84
01:43:10,820 --> 01:43:11,980
So maybe that's what's going on.

85
01:43:11,980 --> 01:43:13,320
All right.

86
01:43:14,320 --> 01:43:17,780
Now once I was in the Johnson Center Library

87
01:43:17,780 --> 01:43:19,740
and I had like 20 minutes to kill,

88
01:43:19,740 --> 01:43:21,660
and I just randomly picked up a book off the shelf called

89
01:43:21,660 --> 01:43:24,860
The ETS's Rank of Error, which was an anti-testing,

90
01:43:24,860 --> 01:43:27,640
educational testing service book by Ralph Nader's organization.

91
01:43:28,300 --> 01:43:30,200
And I clicked on a random page and it said,

92
01:43:30,200 --> 01:43:33,060
do you know these tests perfectly predict GPA only 20%

93
01:43:33,060 --> 01:43:33,540
of the time?

94
01:43:34,920 --> 01:43:37,860
I'm like, they perfectly predict GPA 20% of the time?

95
01:43:37,860 --> 01:43:38,780
That's incredible.

96
01:43:38,780 --> 01:43:41,740
I mean, like how many decimal places?

97
01:43:41,740 --> 01:43:44,120
I mean, like it didn't say, but I was like, wow,

98
01:43:44,120 --> 01:43:45,780
what an incredible endorsement of the test.

99
01:43:45,780 --> 01:43:47,860
Way better than I would have thought.

100
01:43:47,860 --> 01:43:50,900
But of course, the complaint was like 80%

101
01:43:50,900 --> 01:43:52,260
of the time they give a wrong answer.

102
01:43:52,260 --> 01:43:53,340
Well, how wrong?

103
01:43:53,720 --> 01:43:54,660
This is statistics.

104
01:43:54,660 --> 01:43:57,380
It's not math where we expect the answer to be right

105
01:43:57,860 --> 01:43:59,820
to an infinite infinity of decimal places.

106
01:43:59,820 --> 01:44:02,340
It's a real world problem where to be right

107
01:44:02,340 --> 01:44:04,260
to even one decimal place is pretty good.

108
01:44:04,380 --> 01:44:05,560
All right.

109
01:44:06,340 --> 01:44:07,980
So anyway, so all this is true.

110
01:44:07,980 --> 01:44:10,300
Like all those, so many problems with IQ tests.

111
01:44:10,860 --> 01:44:12,400
I mean, it just goes on and on.

112
01:44:12,400 --> 01:44:13,660
Actually, there's a giant literature.

113
01:44:13,660 --> 01:44:15,860
Amazingly, people who are really interested

114
01:44:15,860 --> 01:44:18,500
in IQ spend a lot of time finding out all the problems

115
01:44:18,500 --> 01:44:19,140
and publishing them.

116
01:44:19,760 --> 01:44:21,460
Just like economists, really into markets.

117
01:44:21,520 --> 01:44:23,860
We are the people who have all these complaints about markets.

118
01:44:23,860 --> 01:44:25,620
We can make a career complaining about markets.

119
01:44:26,620 --> 01:44:26,780
All right.

120
01:44:26,780 --> 01:44:30,100
So anyway, important thing is that IQ tests are more reliable

121
01:44:30,100 --> 01:44:32,060
than anything else, especially if you want

122
01:44:32,060 --> 01:44:34,980
to predict someone's income or educational success

123
01:44:34,980 --> 01:44:38,100
or their occupational attainment when they're seven years old.

124
01:44:38,100 --> 01:44:40,240
And you only get one variable.

125
01:44:40,240 --> 01:44:43,700
IQ is the best variable to get.

126
01:44:44,200 --> 01:44:46,320
Does this mean that if you get a low score, you're doomed?

127
01:44:46,320 --> 01:44:46,860
Of course not.

128
01:44:46,860 --> 01:44:49,320
There's plenty of people who do badly

129
01:44:49,320 --> 01:44:52,040
and they just are having a bad day or had some bad luck

130
01:44:52,040 --> 01:44:53,380
and things still work out for them.

131
01:44:53,760 --> 01:44:55,300
And of course, you can get a great score

132
01:44:55,300 --> 01:44:56,500
and still become a total failure.

133
01:44:56,500 --> 01:44:57,620
It's also open to you.

134
01:44:58,120 --> 01:45:01,600
But nevertheless, once you apply the normal standards

135
01:45:01,600 --> 01:45:05,360
of statistics, which is don't expect too much, be grateful

136
01:45:05,360 --> 01:45:08,200
for what we can predict, then IQ tests look

137
01:45:08,200 --> 01:45:09,000
like they're looking pretty good.

138
01:45:09,000 --> 01:45:12,200
So IQ tests are more reliable than anything else.

139
01:45:12,660 --> 01:45:13,880
Very broadly useful.

140
01:45:14,480 --> 01:45:16,860
So there's an enormous number of things

141
01:45:16,860 --> 01:45:17,840
that you can predict with IQ.

142
01:45:17,840 --> 01:45:19,180
All right.

143
01:45:19,180 --> 01:45:21,200
And furthermore, if you test 100 geniuses,

144
01:45:21,200 --> 01:45:22,540
their average score would be very high.

145
01:45:24,160 --> 01:45:28,100
So there's a famous study called, it was done

146
01:45:28,100 --> 01:45:29,860
by a psychologist named Terman.

147
01:45:29,860 --> 01:45:32,420
And then the people in the study were called the Termites.

148
01:45:32,920 --> 01:45:33,660
All right.

149
01:45:33,660 --> 01:45:35,880
Anyway, so what happened was in the state of California

150
01:45:35,880 --> 01:45:39,100
in I think the 20s, there was a massive statewide test.

151
01:45:39,520 --> 01:45:42,160
And then he went and found 100 kids who scored highest

152
01:45:42,160 --> 01:45:44,120
on the test and he studied them for the rest of their lives.

153
01:45:45,160 --> 01:45:47,440
Now, funny thing, there was a Nobel Laureate named William

154
01:45:47,440 --> 01:45:52,300
Shockley who was notorious for his very vocal,

155
01:45:52,300 --> 01:45:53,840
vocally pro IQ views.

156
01:45:54,600 --> 01:45:58,580
And he was tested in this bunch and did not make the cut.

157
01:45:59,100 --> 01:46:02,340
So a Nobel Prize winner was not, did not make it

158
01:46:02,340 --> 01:46:03,340
into the Terman pool.

159
01:46:03,340 --> 01:46:06,180
And then I don't think any of the other 100 ever won

160
01:46:06,180 --> 01:46:06,860
a Nobel Prize.

161
01:46:07,340 --> 01:46:08,700
So stupid test, right?

162
01:46:09,360 --> 01:46:11,420
Well, Terman was like a number 110 in the state.

163
01:46:12,340 --> 01:46:14,580
So yes, he was not in the top 100, he was 110.

164
01:46:15,020 --> 01:46:17,940
But still, he wasn't like number 5 million kid.

165
01:46:18,420 --> 01:46:19,980
He was still near the top.

166
01:46:19,980 --> 01:46:21,460
He just was a little bit worse

167
01:46:21,460 --> 01:46:22,820
than the very best one in the state.

168
01:46:23,060 --> 01:46:25,180
So not so shocking.

169
01:46:26,120 --> 01:46:26,300
All right.

170
01:46:26,300 --> 01:46:27,820
So what can we say about intelligence?

171
01:46:27,820 --> 01:46:29,120
It's a lot like strength.

172
01:46:29,800 --> 01:46:32,140
There's some ambiguity because it's a word that's existed

173
01:46:32,140 --> 01:46:34,260
for thousands of years, like how could we do without it?

174
01:46:34,260 --> 01:46:36,820
It's not going to have mathematical precision.

175
01:46:37,820 --> 01:46:39,820
Right? So there's some ambiguity,

176
01:46:39,820 --> 01:46:41,080
but we do know what we mean.

177
01:46:41,080 --> 01:46:43,960
If you call someone strong, you have an idea what that means.

178
01:46:44,320 --> 01:46:46,060
And there's different observations you can have

179
01:46:46,060 --> 01:46:48,320
where you would say, no, he's not, or he clearly is.

180
01:46:49,300 --> 01:46:51,140
Right? And there's ways that you would try to measure it,

181
01:46:51,140 --> 01:46:52,580
which would make sense to almost anyone

182
01:46:52,660 --> 01:46:53,780
who understands the language.

183
01:46:54,100 --> 01:46:57,340
And since we know what we, so we basically know what we mean.

184
01:46:57,840 --> 01:46:58,940
We know there's real differences.

185
01:46:58,940 --> 01:47:00,340
So some people are stronger than me.

186
01:47:00,340 --> 01:47:01,860
Some people are smarter than me.

187
01:47:02,860 --> 01:47:03,860
Right? That's just a fact.

188
01:47:05,100 --> 01:47:07,380
Right? And also we know that people are strong

189
01:47:07,380 --> 01:47:09,200
by one measure, usually strong by other measures too.

190
01:47:09,980 --> 01:47:11,500
So someone can bench press a lot,

191
01:47:11,500 --> 01:47:14,060
and they probably also can do a lot in a high squat.

192
01:47:14,500 --> 01:47:15,540
They probably have a strong neck.

193
01:47:15,960 --> 01:47:17,260
They probably even have strong fingers.

194
01:47:17,900 --> 01:47:19,480
Now if there were a football player who turned

195
01:47:19,480 --> 01:47:21,500
out to have a really weak pinky or something like that,

196
01:47:21,560 --> 01:47:25,380
would we then say, well sure, he's great on 99 of the tests

197
01:47:25,380 --> 01:47:27,460
of strength, but he has a below average pinky.

198
01:47:27,460 --> 01:47:30,820
Like would you then say he's weak?

199
01:47:30,820 --> 01:47:32,340
I would say no, no, he's a super strong guy

200
01:47:32,340 --> 01:47:33,500
with like a, with a weak pinky.

201
01:47:34,120 --> 01:47:35,740
Yeah, but you know, like you, you know, you would go

202
01:47:35,740 --> 01:47:37,340
and say, all right, let's go and put a little bit of weight

203
01:47:37,340 --> 01:47:40,100
on that so we won't call him quite as strong as we would have

204
01:47:40,400 --> 01:47:42,040
if he also had great pinky strength,

205
01:47:42,700 --> 01:47:44,700
but still he's a strong guy.

206
01:47:46,360 --> 01:47:47,860
Okay. All right.

207
01:47:47,860 --> 01:47:49,600
Now there's a second debate which we are not going

208
01:47:49,600 --> 01:47:52,620
to talk about other than to mention that it exists.

209
01:47:53,200 --> 01:47:55,380
But that's the extent to which IQ is hereditary

210
01:47:55,380 --> 01:47:56,160
or environmental.

211
01:47:56,160 --> 01:47:58,080
You know, what is very well established is

212
01:47:58,080 --> 01:47:59,860
that IQ doesn't change much past a certain age.

213
01:48:01,500 --> 01:48:03,460
You can, it is totally possible

214
01:48:03,460 --> 01:48:06,180
to raise someone's IQ score on a particular test.

215
01:48:06,980 --> 01:48:09,440
But, but this is a, this is a special case,

216
01:48:09,440 --> 01:48:11,380
a more general rule, which is it's possible

217
01:48:11,380 --> 01:48:13,400
to raise anybody's score on any test whatsoever.

218
01:48:14,680 --> 01:48:16,180
How can you raise anybody's score

219
01:48:16,180 --> 01:48:17,860
on anything, on any test whatsoever?

220
01:48:18,960 --> 01:48:19,800
Teach to the test.

221
01:48:20,340 --> 01:48:21,100
Teach to the test.

222
01:48:21,220 --> 01:48:22,100
That's the simple rule.

223
01:48:22,860 --> 01:48:23,120
All right.

224
01:48:23,240 --> 01:48:25,220
So that's the, so like you just want, but,

225
01:48:25,220 --> 01:48:27,360
but there is one problem with teaching the test,

226
01:48:27,360 --> 01:48:29,940
which is if you then go and give them another test,

227
01:48:29,940 --> 01:48:31,520
which is supposed to test the same thing,

228
01:48:31,520 --> 01:48:33,880
but it's not the same kind of test, then you don't see,

229
01:48:33,880 --> 01:48:34,940
then you see very little gain.

230
01:48:35,980 --> 01:48:37,520
So if I go and I prep you for the SAT,

231
01:48:37,520 --> 01:48:39,880
I can raise your SAT score, but that's not going to go

232
01:48:39,880 --> 01:48:41,520
and make you do better on the Wexler test,

233
01:48:41,900 --> 01:48:45,300
or the Wunderlich test, or some other unrelated test.

234
01:48:47,120 --> 01:48:47,320
All right.

235
01:48:47,320 --> 01:48:50,140
So these are what researchers call hollow gains.

236
01:48:50,640 --> 01:48:54,540
We have made you look better just by going and drilling you

237
01:48:54,540 --> 01:48:56,340
on the superficial aspects of the test.

238
01:48:57,060 --> 01:48:58,740
You know, which again is functionally not so different

239
01:48:58,740 --> 01:48:59,900
from us giving you the answers, right?

240
01:49:00,860 --> 01:49:02,640
If someone went and gave you the answer key for a test,

241
01:49:02,640 --> 01:49:05,280
and you memorized it A, B, B, B, C, A, B, C, D,

242
01:49:05,600 --> 01:49:07,720
and you got a perfect score on the test, are you now a genius?

243
01:49:08,580 --> 01:49:09,840
No. It's like, well, why not?

244
01:49:09,840 --> 01:49:11,340
So you got a genius score on the test.

245
01:49:11,340 --> 01:49:12,340
Just doesn't that count?

246
01:49:12,920 --> 01:49:14,360
How would you know it doesn't count?

247
01:49:14,360 --> 01:49:15,720
Well, because those, the gains,

248
01:49:16,160 --> 01:49:18,600
once you've memorized the answer key, aren't going to lead

249
01:49:18,600 --> 01:49:21,320
to better performance on other tests that are supposed

250
01:49:21,320 --> 01:49:23,020
to test the same thing, but where you didn't make it

251
01:49:23,020 --> 01:49:24,800
to memorize in advance.

252
01:49:24,800 --> 01:49:27,000
So anyway, so there's no time to resolve the extent

253
01:49:27,000 --> 01:49:28,760
to which IQ is registered environmental.

254
01:49:29,020 --> 01:49:34,040
We can say it's stable beyond a certain age, but if you go

255
01:49:34,040 --> 01:49:37,080
and read carefully constructed twin adoption studies,

256
01:49:37,520 --> 01:49:40,360
they will say that variance in IQ is about 80% genetic,

257
01:49:41,180 --> 01:49:43,860
and that about 20% is just noise, where it's very hard

258
01:49:43,860 --> 01:49:46,340
to figure out what's going on, and then almost none

259
01:49:46,340 --> 01:49:48,100
of it is explained by the family you're raised by,

260
01:49:48,700 --> 01:49:49,960
at least within rich countries.

261
01:49:50,560 --> 01:49:52,360
These data sets all come from first world countries,

262
01:49:52,360 --> 01:49:55,620
so it's totally possible that if a kid, you take and adopt a kid

263
01:49:55,620 --> 01:49:57,220
from a third world country and bring him to the first world,

264
01:49:57,220 --> 01:50:01,640
he will get a much better score, but if you go and move a kid

265
01:50:01,640 --> 01:50:03,840
from a poor family in a rich country to a rich family

266
01:50:03,840 --> 01:50:05,680
in a rich country, it does not look

267
01:50:05,680 --> 01:50:07,740
like that will actually raise his adult IQ.

268
01:50:09,460 --> 01:50:10,180
Only a messenger.

269
01:50:10,760 --> 01:50:12,100
It will raise his childhood IQ.

270
01:50:12,560 --> 01:50:15,060
How can it, how can being, growing up with a rich family,

271
01:50:15,060 --> 01:50:17,060
raise your childhood IQ, but not your adult IQ?

272
01:50:18,080 --> 01:50:21,040
Well, there's a general, there's a general word

273
01:50:21,040 --> 01:50:24,920
that people use here, which is fade out, fade out.

274
01:50:25,460 --> 01:50:26,840
Remember how you learned a foreign language

275
01:50:26,840 --> 01:50:28,220
in high school, now you don't know it anymore?

276
01:50:29,040 --> 01:50:29,640
That's fade out.

277
01:50:30,360 --> 01:50:33,780
Similarly, if you are put into an advantaged environment

278
01:50:33,780 --> 01:50:36,360
when you're young, and then you don't stay

279
01:50:36,360 --> 01:50:38,580
in that advantaged environment, or of course,

280
01:50:38,580 --> 01:50:40,920
you acquire the ability to control your environment,

281
01:50:41,940 --> 01:50:45,760
then we see that, that, that, then the effect disappears.

282
01:50:46,700 --> 01:50:49,420
We even see fade out for things like religiosity,

283
01:50:49,420 --> 01:50:53,560
where if you are, if you are from atheist stock,

284
01:50:53,560 --> 01:50:54,940
your biological parents are atheists,

285
01:50:54,940 --> 01:50:56,380
but you're adopted by religious people,

286
01:50:56,660 --> 01:50:57,600
when you're 10 years old,

287
01:50:57,600 --> 01:51:00,280
your adopting family will have a big effect upon your

288
01:51:00,280 --> 01:51:00,980
Christian edits.

289
01:51:01,860 --> 01:51:03,920
If we go and look at you by the time you're 35,

290
01:51:04,180 --> 01:51:06,920
and this has been done, then we see very little effect.

291
01:51:07,860 --> 01:51:09,900
Right? What's the difference between a 10-year-old raised

292
01:51:09,900 --> 01:51:11,760
by religious people and a 35-year-old raised

293
01:51:11,760 --> 01:51:12,600
by religious people?

294
01:51:13,240 --> 01:51:18,400
Yeah, you can try, but it's harder.

295
01:51:18,400 --> 01:51:22,280
Yeah. So, people grow up, they do what they want to do,

296
01:51:22,500 --> 01:51:22,840
generally.

297
01:51:24,000 --> 01:51:24,300
All right.

298
01:51:24,600 --> 01:51:26,400
Just like you guys now do a bunch of things

299
01:51:26,400 --> 01:51:27,940
that your parents probably raised you not to do.

300
01:51:28,720 --> 01:51:30,760
Like, I was raised to be economically illiterate.

301
01:51:30,960 --> 01:51:35,440
But, you're breaking my parents' heart.

302
01:51:35,440 --> 01:51:35,940
All right.

303
01:51:35,940 --> 01:51:40,260
So, anyway, so why bring this all up?

304
01:51:41,000 --> 01:51:43,300
Many of you think I bring it all up just to upset people,

305
01:51:43,300 --> 01:51:44,920
which is not my goal at all.

306
01:51:45,500 --> 01:51:47,260
In fact, I would love to make everyone happy.

307
01:51:47,260 --> 01:51:48,960
That is my dream.

308
01:51:48,960 --> 01:51:54,860
I bring it up because when you go and add a measure of IQ

309
01:51:54,860 --> 01:51:56,880
to this list of control variables,

310
01:51:57,220 --> 01:51:59,040
it drastically alters the result.

311
01:51:59,840 --> 01:52:01,180
It drastically alters the result.

312
01:52:01,840 --> 01:52:06,160
So, if you go and just put in the NLSY measure of IQ

313
01:52:06,160 --> 01:52:15,800
into this regression, then beta goes from 12.6 down to 7.5%.

314
01:52:15,800 --> 01:52:19,700
So, the beta is.126, which is a 12.6% rate of return.

315
01:52:20,040 --> 01:52:23,300
Goes down to.075, which is a 7.5% rate of return.

316
01:52:23,660 --> 01:52:27,240
So, that is something like a 40% cut in the payoff,

317
01:52:27,920 --> 01:52:29,900
40% reduction in the effect.

318
01:52:31,120 --> 01:52:32,180
Okay. All right.

319
01:52:32,180 --> 01:52:35,880
Now, another way to think about it is an extra percentile

320
01:52:35,880 --> 01:52:38,300
of IQ bumps you up by about.7% in earnings.

321
01:52:38,900 --> 01:52:40,900
So, a year of education is worth about as much

